registerChapter({
  id: 'local-heap',
  num: '25',
  phase: 'Phase 5 · Locality and the frame rule',
  title: 'Locality of write and free',
  blurb: 'The two commands that change memory, proved local one address at a time. The proofs look identical and spend the disjointness hypothesis in opposite halves: for write it is bookkeeping, and for free it is what makes an equation true.',

  orient: {
    youWill: [
      "Split each locality proof into its two obligations — <i>the frame is still disjoint</i> and <i>the two heaps are equal</i> — before writing a line of Lean.",
      "Prove that <code>Heap.write</code> commutes past a union with <b>no hypothesis at all</b>, and say why that is not a mistake.",
      "Prove that writing preserves disjointness, by using the write rule&rsquo;s own premise to force the frame to be silent at the address written.",
      "Meet the goal <code>⊢ none = hFrame x</code> in the <code>free</code> proof and recognise it as the whole theorem rather than a step in one.",
      "Restate a hypothesis Lean will not rewrite with — <code>have hl' : h l = _ := hl</code> — and say exactly which tactics need the restatement and which do not.",
      "Use <code>show</code> to replace a goal printed through five structure projections by the one-line equation it is, and compose two equations with <code>.trans</code> inside a <code>rw</code> bracket.",
      "Refute locality for <code>free</code> when the frame is allowed to own the freed cell, with two one-cell heaps."
    ],
    needs: [
      "Unit 24: <code>HeapLocal</code>, its five components, and the shape <code>intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex</code> followed by an inversion.",
      "Unit 09: <code>union_of_none</code> and <code>union_of_some</code>, and that <code>Heap.union</code> answers from its left argument wherever that argument is defined.",
      "Unit 05: <code>write_same</code>, <code>write_other</code>, <code>erase_same</code>, <code>erase_other</code>. Unit 06: the three-move shape <code>funext</code>, split on the point, close both branches.",
      "Unit 19: <code>Exec.write</code> and <code>Exec.free</code> each carry a premise saying the heap they run on holds something at <code>l</code>."
    ],
    payoff: "These are the two hardest pointwise heap arguments in the corpus, and the last for a long while: nothing in the rest of this module opens a heap at an address, and the next proof that does is five units away. They are also the first evidence that the extra conjunct Unit 24 derived is payable by a command that changes the heap — the three commands already known to be local all hand it back untouched."
  },

  blocks: [

    /* ============================================ what has to be proved === */

    {t:'p', h:"Two commands, then, and both of them change the heap. A <code>write</code> replaces one cell; a <code>free</code> removes one. The shape of the two proofs is going to be identical — <code>intro</code>, one inversion, two obligations, and a <code>funext</code> underneath — and the mathematics in them is not identical at all. Working out where they differ, before writing any Lean, is most of the work."},

    {t:'p', h:"Start with <code>write</code>. After <code>intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex</code> and an inversion on <code>hex</code>, the arbitrary final state <code>s'</code> has been replaced by the state <code>Exec.write</code> produces: the store is untouched and the heap is <code>Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)</code>. The inversion also leaves behind the rule&rsquo;s premise, <code>h l = some old</code> for a value <code>old</code> whose name Lean will not let you type. So the goal is a conjunction whose two halves are these."},

    {t:'steps', title:'The two obligations, before any Lean',
     items:[
       {k:'The frame is still disjoint', h:"<code>Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)</code> does not overlap <code>hFrame</code>. You are given that <code>h</code> does not overlap it. Writing at <code>l</code> can only add <code>l</code> to the domain, so the only address at which the claim could fail is <code>l</code> — and the write rule&rsquo;s premise says <code>h</code> already holds something there, so the frame does not."},
       {k:'The two heaps are equal', h:"Writing to the combined heap gives the same heap as writing to <code>h</code> and then putting the frame beside it. Two functions, so: fix an address and compare. At <code>l</code> both sides answer <code>some v</code>. Away from <code>l</code> neither side has changed anything, so both sides answer whatever the combined heap answered."}
     ]},

    {t:'p', h:"There are two more components in the tuple — the derivation on the big heap, and the equation between the stores — and neither has content. The store is untouched by a write, so its equation is <code>rfl</code>. The derivation is <code>Exec.write</code> applied to a premise about the big heap, and <code>union_of_some</code> turns the premise you have into the premise you need — the same move that was all of <code>heapLocal_load</code>. The <code>free</code> proof pays both slots the same way, and neither proof is about them."},

    {t:'p', h:"Now read the second obligation again. Nothing in it mentions disjointness. The argument given for it — at <code>l</code> both sides answer <code>some v</code>, elsewhere nothing changed — never asks who owns what. That is not an oversight in the sketch; the equation is true for <i>any</i> two heaps, overlapping or not, and the corpus proves it separately with no hypothesis to spend. Meanwhile the first obligation is exactly where the disjointness hypothesis and the write rule&rsquo;s premise are both consumed."},

    {t:'p', h:"For <code>free</code> the split lands the other way round, and that is the reason these two proofs are on the same page. Erasing a cell can only shrink a domain, so <i>the frame is still disjoint</i> costs nothing at all. The equation is where the argument lives: at the erased address the left-hand side answers <code>none</code>, and the right-hand side answers whatever <code>hFrame</code> holds there. Those agree only because the frame holds nothing there, which is a fact about disjointness and about the free rule&rsquo;s premise together."},

    {t:'tbl', cap:"Where each proof spends what it was given. <code>hd</code> is the disjointness hypothesis; <code>hl</code> is the premise carried by the rule that fired — <code>h l = some old</code> for <code>write</code>, <code>h l = some v</code> for <code>free</code>. Two obligations, two commands, and the hypotheses sit in opposite cells.",
     head:['', 'the frame is still disjoint', 'the two heaps are equal'],
     rows:[
       ['<code>write l e</code>', 'both <code>hd</code> and <code>hl</code>', '<b>nothing</b>'],
       ['<code>free l</code>', '<b>nothing</b> beyond <code>hd</code>&rsquo;s own statement', 'both <code>hd</code> and <code>hl</code>']
     ]},

    {t:'p', h:"So one of these proofs uses disjointness for bookkeeping — to hand back a fact of the same kind it was given — and the other uses it to make an equation true. That distinction is the content of this unit, and it decides which of the two commands can have its locality broken by a bad frame."},

    /* =============================================== the write equation === */

    {t:'sec', s:'The equation, with nothing assumed'},

    {t:'p', h:"Unit 06 proved that two writes at different addresses commute, and it needed the disequality handed to it. This is the same statement with the second write replaced by a whole heap: instead of <i>the other write is somewhere else</i>, the claim is <i>the other heap is beside you</i>. It opens the same way and it needs no hypothesis."},

    {t:'code', tag:'verified', cap:"In the corpus this proof also appears inline, inside <code>heapLocal_write</code>. Having it separately is what lets you check the claim that no hypothesis is needed: there is nowhere for one to hide.",
     src:"theorem write_union_no_disjointness (h hFrame : Heap) (l : Loc) (v : Val) :\n    Heap.write (Heap.union h hFrame) l v = Heap.union (Heap.write h l v) hFrame := by\n  funext x\n  by_cases hx : x = l\n  · subst hx\n    rw [write_same, union_of_some hFrame (write_same h x v)]\n  · rw [write_other (Heap.union h hFrame) l x v hx]\n    have hw : Heap.write h l v x = h x := write_other h l x v hx\n    cases hh : h x with\n    | none => rw [union_of_none hFrame hh, union_of_none hFrame (hw.trans hh)]\n    | some w => rw [union_of_some hFrame hh, union_of_some hFrame (hw.trans hh)]"},

    {t:'trace', title:'write_union_no_disjointness, address by address',
     start:'⊢ (h.union hFrame).write l v = (h.write l v).union hFrame',
     steps:[
       {tac:'funext x',
        state:"h hFrame : Heap\nl : Loc\nv : Val\nx : Loc\n⊢ (h.union hFrame).write l v x = (h.write l v).union hFrame x",
        h:"An equation between two heaps became an equation between two lookups at an arbitrary address, and gave you <code>x</code> to split on. Every heap equation in the course starts here."},
       {tac:'by_cases hx : x = l  ·  subst hx',
        state:"case pos\nh hFrame : Heap\nv : Val\nx : Loc\n⊢ (h.union hFrame).write x v x = (h.write x v).union hFrame x",
        h:"The positive branch, with <code>l</code> substituted away so that both sides are lookups at the address the write targeted. <code>subst</code> replaces <code>l</code> by <code>x</code> rather than the other way round, which is why the display now says <code>write x v x</code>."},
       {tac:'rw [write_same]',
        state:"case pos\nh hFrame : Heap\nv : Val\nx : Loc\n⊢ some v = (h.write x v).union hFrame x",
        h:"The left-hand side is a write read back at the address written, whatever heap is underneath. That is <code>write_same</code>, and it does not care that the heap underneath is a union."},
       {tac:'rw [union_of_some hFrame (write_same h x v)]',
        state:'No goals.',
        h:"The right-hand side is a union whose left argument is defined here — <code>write_same h x v</code> is the proof that it is — so the union answers from the left, and it answers <code>some v</code>. The two sides are now literally the same term and <code>rw</code>&rsquo;s trailing <code>rfl</code> closes the branch. The corpus writes these two rewrites in one bracket."},
       {tac:'· rw [write_other (Heap.union h hFrame) l x v hx]',
        state:"case neg\nh hFrame : Heap\nl : Loc\nv : Val\nx : Loc\nhx : ¬x = l\n⊢ h.union hFrame x = (h.write l v).union hFrame x",
        h:"The negative branch. Reading a write at some other address gives whatever was there before, so the left-hand side loses its write entirely. The right-hand side keeps one, because there the write is buried inside a union and no lookup law reaches it in one step."},
       {tac:'cases hh : h x with | none => …',
        state:"case neg.none\nh hFrame : Heap\nl : Loc\nv : Val\nx : Loc\nhx : ¬x = l\nhw : h.write l v x = h x\nhh : h x = none\n⊢ h.union hFrame x = (h.write l v).union hFrame x",
        h:"Both remaining unions have to be resolved, and each needs to know whether its left argument is defined at <code>x</code>. Splitting on the value of <code>h x</code> answers that for the left union directly, and for the right one through <code>hw</code>. The saved equation <code>hh</code> is the whole point of this form of <code>cases</code>: without it the branch would know it was in the <code>none</code> case and have nothing to rewrite with."}
     ],
     done:'No goals.'},

    {t:'p', h:"The last line of each branch is where two equations get composed. <code>hh : h x = none</code> is about <code>h</code>; the right-hand union needs the same fact about <code>Heap.write h l v</code>, and <code>hw</code> says those two agree at <code>x</code>. Writing <code>hw.trans hh</code> chains them into a single proof of <code>Heap.write h l v x = none</code>, which is what <code>union_of_none</code> wants as its argument. It is <code>Eq.trans hw hh</code> in dot notation, resolved as always by the head of <code>hw</code>&rsquo;s type — and building the term inline saves naming a hypothesis that is used once, in the bracket where it is built."},

    {t:'p', h:"Nothing in that proof asks whether <code>h</code> and <code>hFrame</code> overlap. If they do, <code>Heap.union h hFrame</code> hides some of the frame&rsquo;s cells behind <code>h</code>&rsquo;s — and the equation holds anyway, because writing at <code>l</code> changes exactly one address on both sides and both unions are biased towards the same heap. Whatever was hidden stays hidden, in the same way, on both sides of the equation."},

    /* ========================================= the write disjointness === */

    {t:'sec', s:'Where the write proof does spend its hypotheses'},

    {t:'p', h:"The other obligation is <code>Heap.disjoint (Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)) hFrame</code>. Unpacked, that is: at every address, one of the two heaps is undefined. Fix an address <code>x</code>. You know <code>h x = none</code> or <code>hFrame x = none</code>, and you have to produce the same disjunction with the write in front."},

    {t:'p', h:"If <code>x</code> is not <code>l</code>, the write is invisible there and the disjunction you were given is the disjunction you want. If <code>x</code> is <code>l</code>, the left disjunct is false — the write put a value there — so the second disjunct had better hold, and the case where you were given the first one has to be impossible. It is: the premise of the write rule says <code>h l = some old</code>, and <code>h l = none</code> contradicts it. That is the whole argument, and it is the reason a command may only write where it owns."},

    {t:'p', h:"Take away the premise and the claim is not unproved but false."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:"One address, two heaps, and a write at an address the writer does not own. Both disjuncts fail at 0: the write put <code>some 1</code> there and the frame holds <code>some 9</code>. If <code>Exec.write</code> had no premise, this would be a command that destroys the frame it was handed.",
     src:"theorem write_breaks_disjointness :\n    ¬ Heap.disjoint (Heap.write Heap.empty 0 1) (Heap.singleton 0 9) := by\n  intro hd\n  rcases hd 0 with hx | hx\n  · rw [write_same] at hx; exact absurd hx (by simp)\n  · rw [singleton_same] at hx; exact absurd hx (by simp)"},

    /* ========================================== restating the premise === */

    {t:'h4', s:'A hypothesis Lean will not rewrite with'},

    {t:'p', h:"The argument above turns on one rewrite: <code>h x = none</code> and <code>h x = some old</code> cannot both hold, so rewrite one into the other and read off the contradiction. The premise the inversion left you is not in a shape <code>rw</code> can use. <code>Exec.write</code>&rsquo;s premise is stated about <code>s.heap</code> for the state the rule was applied at, and that state is the literal <code>⟨σ, h⟩</code>, so the hypothesis reads <code>hl : { store := σ, heap := h }.heap l = some old✝</code>."},

    {t:'code', tag:'sketch', cap:"The write proof with <code>hl</code> used directly. Everything else about it is right.",
     src:"          · subst hxl; rw [hl] at hx; exact absurd hx (by simp)"},

    {t:'state', cap:"What Lean says, with the <code>snippet:L:C:</code> prefix dropped — the convention for every message and every goal state on this page — and with the goal display Lean reprints underneath it left off, since nothing in it has moved. <code>rw</code> looks for its left-hand side as a <i>term</i>, and <code>{ store := σ, heap := h }.heap x</code> is not the term <code>h x</code>, however plainly the two describe the same lookup.",
     src:"error: Tactic `rewrite` failed: Did not find an occurrence of the pattern\n  { store := σ, heap := h }.heap x\nin the target expression\n  h x = none"},

    {t:'p', h:"They are the same lookup: a projection out of a structure literal reduces to the field. So a proof of one <i>is</i> a proof of the other, and anything that compares types up to reduction will take it. <code>union_of_some hFrame hl</code> typechecks exactly as it stands; only <code>rw</code>, which matches syntax rather than meaning, refuses. The repair is to assert the shape you want and offer the proof you have."},

    {t:'code', tag:'verified', cap:"The line that fixes it, quoted from <code>heapLocal_write</code>. The type declared is the one <code>rw</code> has to see; the term offered is the one the inversion gave you.",
     src:"      have hl' : h l = _ := hl"},

    {t:'p', h:"The underscore is doing real work. The right-hand side is <code>some old✝</code>, and <code>old✝</code> is inaccessible — the inversion introduced it and you may not type its name. Writing <code>_</code> leaves that half of the type for Lean to fill in from <code>hl</code>, while the half you care about, the left-hand side, is stated the way <code>rw</code> needs to see it. The result is <code>hl' : h l = some old✝</code>, which is the same proposition as <code>hl</code> and a different term, and the difference is the entire reason the line exists."},

    /* ================================================ naming the goal === */

    {t:'sec', s:'Naming the last obligation'},

    {t:'p', h:"With both obligations in hand the tuple can be assembled: the disjointness, the state the big run reaches, the derivation, <code>rfl</code> for the stores, and a hole for the equation. The hole comes back printed through the state literals that built it, and unreadable for that reason alone — the mathematics in it is one line."},

    {t:'trace', title:'What refine leaves, and what show makes of it',
     start:'the last hole of the tuple',
     steps:[
       {tac:'refine ⟨hdw, ⟨σ, Heap.write (Heap.union h hFrame) l (e.eval σ)⟩,\n        Exec.write (union_of_some hFrame hl\'), rfl, ?_⟩',
        state:"case write\n⊢ { store := σ, heap := (h.union hFrame).write l (Atom.eval σ e) }.heap =\n    { store := { store := σ, heap := h }.store,\n            heap := { store := σ, heap := h }.heap.write l (Atom.eval { store := σ, heap := h }.store e) }.heap.union\n      hFrame",
        h:"Nine lines of context are omitted here and unchanged; the fold below prints this state in full. What is left is one equation between two heaps, written through five structure projections that will never reduce in the display because nothing asks them to."},
       {tac:'show Heap.write (Heap.union h hFrame) l (e.eval σ)\n       = Heap.union (Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)) hFrame',
        state:"case write\n⊢ (h.union hFrame).write l (Atom.eval σ e) = (h.write l (Atom.eval σ e)).union hFrame",
        h:"The same goal, restated. <code>show</code> accepts any type definitionally equal to the current one, and stripping a projection from a structure literal is exactly that kind of equality. Nothing was proved by this line and nothing was hidden by it: the tactic is in the proof, the reader can type it, and the goal it produces is <code>write_union_no_disjointness</code>&rsquo;s statement with <code>e.eval σ</code> for <code>v</code>."}
     ],
     done:'the equation, ready for funext'},

    {t:'detail', title:'The goal after refine, with the whole context', tag:'aside', open:false,
     blocks:[
       {t:'p', h:"The nine lines the trace above leaves out. They are identical after the <code>show</code>, which is the point: the tactic changes the goal and touches nothing else."},
       {t:'state', cap:"After the <code>refine</code>, in full. The mathematics is one equation; the display is four lines of it and nine of context.",
        src:"case write\nl : Loc\ne : Atom\nσ : Store\nh hFrame : Heap\nhd : h.disjoint hFrame\nold✝ : Val\nhl : { store := σ, heap := h }.heap l = some old✝\nhl' : h l = some old✝\nhdw : (h.write l (Atom.eval σ e)).disjoint hFrame\n⊢ { store := σ, heap := (h.union hFrame).write l (Atom.eval σ e) }.heap =\n    { store := { store := σ, heap := h }.store,\n            heap := { store := σ, heap := h }.heap.write l (Atom.eval { store := σ, heap := h }.store e) }.heap.union\n      hFrame"},
       {t:'p', h:"Two things in that display are worth being able to read. <code>Atom.eval σ e</code> is spelled out rather than printed as <code>e.eval σ</code>, because <code>Atom.eval</code>&rsquo;s first explicit argument is the store and not the expression. And <code>old✝</code> appears only in the two hypotheses about <code>l</code>, never in the goal: the write rule binds the old value in its premise and discards it in its conclusion, which is why nothing later in the proof needs a name for it."}
     ]},

    /* ================================================= exercise m8-2 === */

    {t:'ex',
     id:'m8-2',
     name:'heapLocal_write',
     hard:true,
     why:"The first locality proof with anything in it, and the first evidence that Unit 24&rsquo;s extra conjunct is payable rather than only well argued for. Unit 26 takes locality of the parts as a hypothesis and proves locality of <code>;;</code>, <code>ite</code> and <code>loop</code> from it; Unit 27 turns the whole collection into the frame rule. Both are theorems about a property that, until this proof exists, exactly three commands are known to have — and all three of them leave the heap alone. Downstream this theorem is cited by name three times: once in Unit 27&rsquo;s first worked application of the frame rule, once in Unit 28 where the debt from Unit 00 is paid, and once in Unit 29.",
     setup:"Everything you need is above. The equation half is <code>write_union_no_disjointness</code>, whose proof you have already watched; the corpus writes it out inline, after a <code>show</code>, rather than citing the lemma. The disjointness half is yours: <code>have hdw : Heap.disjoint (Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)) hFrame := by …</code> before the <code>refine</code>, so that the tuple has something to put in its first slot. Prove the two separately and assemble; that decomposition is the point of the exercise, not a concession.",
     goal:"theorem heapLocal_write (l : Loc) (e : Atom) : HeapLocal (.write l e) := by",
     hints:[
       "Unfolded, with nothing yet done to it: for every <code>σ</code>, <code>h</code>, <code>hFrame</code> and <code>s'</code>, if <code>hFrame</code> is disjoint from <code>h</code> and <code>write l e</code> takes <code>⟨σ, h⟩</code> to <code>s'</code>, then two things hold. First, <code>s'.heap</code> is still disjoint from <code>hFrame</code>. Second, there is a state <code>r</code> that the same command reaches from <code>⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩</code>, with <code>r.store = s'.store</code> and <code>r.heap = Heap.union s'.heap hFrame</code> — five components in all, counting the derivation and the two equations separately. Nothing in that statement describes <code>s'</code>: every occurrence of it is a projection out of a state nobody has looked inside.",
       "<code>Exec</code> has exactly one rule that can produce a run of <code>write l e</code>, so <code>s'</code> is not arbitrary: its store is <code>σ</code> and its heap is <code>h</code> with <code>l</code> set to <code>e.eval σ</code>. The same rule hands you its premise — <code>h</code> already holds something at <code>l</code>. With <code>s'</code> known the goal splits in two. Disjointness: fix an address and split on whether it is <code>l</code>. Away from <code>l</code> the write is invisible and the disjunction you were handed still holds. At <code>l</code> the write is not invisible, and the case has to be shown impossible: the premise says <code>h</code> holds a value at <code>l</code>, so the branch in which <code>h l = none</code> cannot arise. Equation: both sides answer <code>some (e.eval σ)</code> at <code>l</code> and neither has changed anything elsewhere — and, as the section above showed, no hypothesis is consulted anywhere in it.",
       "<code>cases hex with | write hl =&gt;</code>, then <code>have hl' : h l = _ := hl</code> so that <code>rw</code> can use it. The disjointness proof is <code>intro x</code>, <code>rcases hd x</code>, <code>by_cases hxl : x = l</code>, and in the impossible branch <code>subst</code>, one <code>rw … at</code>, and <code>absurd</code>. The derivation slot needs <code>union_of_some</code>. The equation is <code>funext</code> and a split, closed by <code>write_same</code>, <code>write_other</code>, <code>union_of_some</code> and <code>union_of_none</code>.",
       "<code>intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex</code>, then <code>cases hex with | write hl =&gt;</code>, then <code>have hl' : h l = _ := hl</code>. That leaves the conjunction with <code>hl'</code> in a usable shape. The next line is <code>have hdw : Heap.disjoint (Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)) hFrame := by</code> and the sub-proof opens <code>intro x</code> / <code>rcases hd x with hx | hx</code>. Once <code>hdw</code> exists, <code>refine ⟨hdw, ⟨σ, Heap.write (Heap.union h hFrame) l (e.eval σ)⟩, Exec.write (union_of_some hFrame hl'), rfl, ?_⟩</code> leaves one goal, and <code>show</code> puts it in the shape the trace above ends on."
     ],
     sol:"theorem heapLocal_write (l : Loc) (e : Atom) : HeapLocal (.write l e) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  cases hex with\n  | write hl =>\n      have hl' : h l = _ := hl\n      have hdw : Heap.disjoint (Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)) hFrame := by\n        intro x\n        rcases hd x with hx | hx\n        · by_cases hxl : x = l\n          · subst hxl; rw [hl'] at hx; exact absurd hx (by simp)\n          · left; rw [write_other h l x (e.eval σ) hxl]; exact hx\n        · exact Or.inr hx\n      refine ⟨hdw, ⟨σ, Heap.write (Heap.union h hFrame) l (e.eval σ)⟩,\n              Exec.write (union_of_some hFrame hl'), rfl, ?_⟩\n      show Heap.write (Heap.union h hFrame) l (e.eval σ)\n             = Heap.union (Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)) hFrame\n      funext x\n      by_cases hxl : x = l\n      · subst hxl\n        rw [write_same (Heap.union h hFrame) x (e.eval σ),\n            union_of_some hFrame (write_same h x (e.eval σ))]\n      · rw [write_other (Heap.union h hFrame) l x (e.eval σ) hxl]\n        have hwx : Heap.write h l (e.eval σ) x = h x := write_other h l x (e.eval σ) hxl\n        cases hx : h x with\n        | none => rw [union_of_none hFrame hx, union_of_none hFrame (hwx.trans hx)]\n        | some w => rw [union_of_some hFrame hx, union_of_some hFrame (hwx.trans hx)]",
     solNote:"Twenty-six lines, of which the last ten are the equation you were shown, spelled out. If you climbed all four hints and are still stuck, open this and read it against the two obligations rather than top to bottom: lines 6–12 are the disjointness half, lines 17–26 the equation, and lines 13–16 are the join.",
     expl:"The proof is the decomposition, written down. The inversion turns an arbitrary <code>s'</code> into the state the write rule produces, which is what makes both obligations concrete. <code>hl'</code> restates the rule&rsquo;s premise so that <code>rw</code> can use it, and it is used twice — once in the impossible branch of the disjointness proof, once as the argument to <code>union_of_some</code> in the derivation. The <code>show</code> replaces a goal written through five projections by the equation it is. Everything after it is the proof of <code>write_union_no_disjointness</code>, line for line, with <code>e.eval σ</code> for <code>v</code>, different names on the hypotheses, and the lemmas&rsquo; arguments written out where the shorter proof left them to be inferred.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'intro σ h hFrame s\' hd hex', h:"Discharged the four binders and the two implications of <code>HeapLocal</code>. The goal is now the conjunction, with <code>s'</code> an arbitrary state nobody has described."},
       {tac:'cases hex with | write hl =>', h:"Inversion. <code>Exec (.write l e)</code> has exactly one rule, so there is one branch, and in it <code>s'</code> is replaced by the state that rule produces. The heap in the goal is now <code>Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)</code>, written through projections. The named <code>hl</code> is the rule&rsquo;s premise; the value it mentions is inaccessible."},
       {tac:'have hl\' : h l = _ := hl', h:"Entered the same proposition into the context under a type <code>rw</code> can match. Nothing was proved; a term changed its declared type to a definitionally equal one. Without this line the rewrite three lines down fails with <code>Did not find an occurrence of the pattern</code>."},
       {tac:'have hdw : Heap.disjoint (Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)) hFrame := by', h:"Opened the first obligation as a separate goal. Stating it in full costs a line and buys the rest of the proof a name for it — the alternative is to leave it as the first hole of the <code>refine</code> and prove it inside a tuple, where the goal display carries the whole conjunction around it."},
       {tac:'intro x', h:"<code>Heap.disjoint</code> is a <code>∀</code> over addresses, so it opens under <code>intro</code>. The goal became a disjunction at one address."},
       {tac:'rcases hd x with hx | hx', h:"Used the hypothesis at the same address, producing two branches: <code>h x = none</code>, or <code>hFrame x = none</code>. The second branch is finished immediately — the write does not touch the frame — which is why the proof spends no more lines on it."},
       {tac:'by_cases hxl : x = l', h:"Inside the first branch, split on whether this is the address written. The two sub-branches are answered in completely different ways — one by a law, one by a contradiction — so the split has to be made in the open."},
       {tac:'subst hxl; rw [hl\'] at hx; exact absurd hx (by simp)', h:"The impossible case. <code>subst</code> replaces <code>l</code> by <code>x</code> everywhere, so <code>hl'</code> now says <code>h x = some old✝</code> while <code>hx</code> says <code>h x = none</code>. Rewriting the first into the second turns <code>hx</code> into <code>some old✝ = none</code>, and <code>absurd</code> with a proof of its negation closes a goal of any shape — which matters here, because the goal is still the whole disjunction and neither disjunct is true."},
       {tac:'left; rw [write_other h l x (e.eval σ) hxl]; exact hx', h:"The other case. Here the left disjunct <i>is</i> the one that holds, so committing to it first is safe; then <code>write_other</code> — whose last argument is exactly the <code>x ≠ l</code> that <code>by_cases</code> produced — removes the write, and the goal is the hypothesis."},
       {tac:'exact Or.inr hx', h:"The frame branch of the <code>rcases</code>, closed without looking at the write at all."},
       {tac:'refine ⟨hdw, ⟨σ, …⟩, Exec.write (union_of_some hFrame hl\'), rfl, ?_⟩', h:"Four of the five components at once. The state claimed for the big run is <code>⟨σ, Heap.write (Heap.union h hFrame) l (e.eval σ)⟩</code> — the store unchanged, the heap being the big heap after the same write. <code>union_of_some hFrame hl'</code> is the premise <code>Exec.write</code> needs about the big heap, derived from the premise you have about the small one: what the union answers at <code>l</code> is what <code>h</code> answers there. The <code>rfl</code> closes the store equation."},
       {tac:'show Heap.write (Heap.union h hFrame) l (e.eval σ) = …', h:"Replaced a goal printed through five structure projections by the one-line equation it already was. From here the proof is the lemma proved above."},
       {tac:'funext x', h:"Turned the equation between two heaps into an equation between two lookups at an arbitrary address, and put <code>x</code> in the context to split on. This is the only move that gets inside a heap at all: <code>Heap</code> is a function type, and there is no other way to compare two of them."},
       {tac:'by_cases hxl : x = l; subst hxl', h:"Split the address into the one the write targeted and all the others, because the two lookup laws that resolve a write are exactly this split. <code>subst</code> then removes <code>l</code> in favour of <code>x</code> throughout, which is why every display below this line says <code>write x v x</code> rather than <code>write l v l</code>."},
       {tac:'rw [write_same …, union_of_some hFrame (write_same h x (e.eval σ))]', h:"The <code>x = l</code> branch. Both sides are made to read <code>some (e.eval σ)</code>: the left because a write reads back what it wrote, whatever heap is underneath, the right because the union&rsquo;s left argument is defined there — <code>write_same h x (e.eval σ)</code> is the proof — and so answers the same thing. The two sides are now the same term and <code>rw</code>&rsquo;s trailing <code>rfl</code> closes the branch."},
       {tac:'rw [write_other (Heap.union h hFrame) l x (e.eval σ) hxl]', h:"The <code>x ≠ l</code> branch. Stripped the write off the left-hand side entirely: read anywhere but the address written, a write answers whatever was there before, so the goal&rsquo;s left side becomes <code>h.union hFrame x</code>. The right-hand side keeps its write, because there it sits inside a union and no single lookup law reaches it."},
       {tac:'have hwx : Heap.write h l (e.eval σ) x = h x := write_other h l x (e.eval σ) hxl', h:"The same law again, at the same address, but about the small heap rather than the union — the fact that the surviving write on the right is invisible too. It has to be a <code>have</code> and not a rewrite: try <code>rw [write_other h l x (e.eval σ) hxl]</code> here and it fails, reporting that the pattern <code>h.write l v x</code> does not occur in <code>h.union hFrame x = (h.write l v).union hFrame x</code>. It does not, because the write is applied to nothing — it sits under the union as an argument, and only the union is applied to <code>x</code>. Saved as a term instead, it is exactly what <code>.trans</code> takes."},
       {tac:'cases hx : h x with | none => … | some w => …', h:"Both sides are now unions, and a union cannot be resolved without knowing whether its left argument is defined here — so split on what <code>h x</code> is, and keep the answer as <code>hx</code>. Without the <code>hx :</code> the branches would know which case they were in and have no equation to rewrite with. <code>hwx.trans hx</code> then transports that answer from <code>h</code> to <code>Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)</code>, which is what the right-hand union needs."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'trace', title:'The disjointness obligation, tactic by tactic',
        start:"⊢ (h.write l (Atom.eval σ e)).disjoint hFrame",
        steps:[
          {tac:'intro x',
           state:"x : Loc\n⊢ h.write l (Atom.eval σ e) x = none ∨ hFrame x = none",
           h:"Context lines above <code>x</code> are omitted here and in the steps below, except where a step changes one; there are eight of them — <code>l</code>, <code>e</code>, <code>σ</code>, <code>h hFrame</code>, <code>hd</code>, <code>old✝</code>, <code>hl</code> and <code>hl'</code>, in that order. <code>Heap.disjoint</code> unfolds under <code>intro</code> with no tactic naming it, because it is a <code>∀</code> by definition."},
          {tac:'rcases hd x with hx | hx',
           state:"case inl\nx : Loc\nhx : h x = none\n⊢ h.write l (Atom.eval σ e) x = none ∨ hFrame x = none",
           h:"The first of two branches. The goal has not changed; what changed is that there is now something in the context about this address."},
          {tac:'by_cases hxl : x = l',
           state:"case pos\nx : Loc\nhx : h x = none\nhxl : x = l\n⊢ h.write l (Atom.eval σ e) x = none ∨ hFrame x = none",
           h:"Still no progress on the goal, and none is possible: at this point both disjuncts are open questions. The hypotheses are what will settle it."},
          {tac:'subst hxl',
           state:"case pos\nx : Loc\nhx : h x = none\nhl : { store := σ, heap := h }.heap x = some old✝\nhl' : h x = some old✝\n⊢ h.write x (Atom.eval σ e) x = none ∨ hFrame x = none",
           h:"<code>l</code> is gone and <code>x</code> stands in its place everywhere, including in both statements of the rule&rsquo;s premise — which is why <code>hl</code> is shown here beside <code>hl'</code>, in the two spellings the section above compared. The context now holds two statements about <code>h x</code> that cannot both be true. This is the step at which the branch becomes vacuous, though nothing on the screen says so yet."},
          {tac:'rw [hl\'] at hx',
           state:"case pos\nx : Loc\nhx : some old✝ = none\nhl : { store := σ, heap := h }.heap x = some old✝\nhl' : h x = some old✝\n⊢ h.write x (Atom.eval σ e) x = none ∨ hFrame x = none",
           h:"The contradiction, in the context, in one line. Rewriting with <code>hl'</code> inside <code>hx</code> replaces <code>h x</code> by <code>some old✝</code>. Had the restatement not been made, this is the line that fails."},
          {tac:'exact absurd hx (by simp)',
           state:'No goals.',
           h:"<code>absurd</code> takes a proof and a proof of its negation and returns anything, so it closes a disjunction neither of whose sides holds. <code>by simp</code> supplies <code>¬ (some old✝ = none)</code> from constructor distinctness."}
        ],
        done:'No goals.'},
       {t:'detail', title:'The goal you actually face, and a way to make it readable at once', tag:'aside', open:false,
        blocks:[
          {t:'p', h:"None of the traces on this page starts from what the inversion leaves, because what the inversion leaves is this."},
          {t:'state', cap:"After <code>cases hex with | write hl =&gt;</code>, in full. Twenty lines, of which the mathematics is: the heap gained a cell at <code>l</code>, the store did not change, and two things have to be produced. Every occurrence of the state the rule built is printed as a literal with its projection still on it, five times.",
           src:"case write\nl : Loc\ne : Atom\nσ : Store\nh hFrame : Heap\nhd : h.disjoint hFrame\nold✝ : Val\nhl : { store := σ, heap := h }.heap l = some old✝\n⊢ { store := { store := σ, heap := h }.store,\n            heap := { store := σ, heap := h }.heap.write l (Atom.eval { store := σ, heap := h }.store e) }.heap.disjoint\n      hFrame ∧\n    ∃ r,\n      Exec (Cmd.write l e) { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r ∧\n        r.store =\n            { store := { store := σ, heap := h }.store,\n                heap := { store := σ, heap := h }.heap.write l (Atom.eval { store := σ, heap := h }.store e) }.store ∧\n          r.heap =\n            { store := { store := σ, heap := h }.store,\n                    heap :=\n                      { store := σ, heap := h }.heap.write l (Atom.eval { store := σ, heap := h }.store e) }.heap.union\n              hFrame"},
          {t:'p', h:"The corpus proof works under that display and reaches for <code>show</code> only at the end, on the last hole. Nothing stops you doing it at the start, on the whole conjunction."},
          {t:'code', tag:'sketch', cap:"An extract: the opening of the proof with a <code>show</code> inserted immediately after the inversion. It needs the rest of the proof under it to compile.",
           src:"  cases hex with\n  | write hl =>\n      show Heap.disjoint (Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)) hFrame ∧\n           ∃ r : State,\n             Exec (.write l e) ⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩ r ∧\n             r.store = σ ∧\n             r.heap = Heap.union (Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)) hFrame"},
          {t:'state', cap:"What that leaves. Twelve lines for twenty, and the two obligations are now readable side by side: the conjunct on the first line, the equation on the last.",
           src:"case write\nl : Loc\ne : Atom\nσ : Store\nh hFrame : Heap\nhd : h.disjoint hFrame\nold✝ : Val\nhl : { store := σ, heap := h }.heap l = some old✝\n⊢ (h.write l (Atom.eval σ e)).disjoint hFrame ∧\n    ∃ r,\n      Exec (Cmd.write l e) { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r ∧\n        r.store = σ ∧ r.heap = (h.write l (Atom.eval σ e)).union hFrame"},
          {t:'p', h:"The cost is five lines of restatement that prove nothing, and they have to be kept correct by hand if the definition ever changes. The corpus pays for readability only where the display is actually in the way, which is the last hole; the first obligation gets a readable statement anyway, from the <code>have hdw</code> that proves it."}
        ]},
       {t:'p', h:"The other branch of the <code>by_cases</code> takes two states and no surprises: <code>left</code> commits to the write&rsquo;s side, <code>rw [write_other h l x (e.eval σ) hxl]</code> turns the goal <code>h.write l (Atom.eval σ e) x = none</code> into <code>h x = none</code>, and <code>exact hx</code> closes it."},
       {t:'p', h:"The corpus proves the equation where it stands. Cite it instead and everything below the <code>refine</code> disappears."},
       {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:"The same theorem with the equation cited rather than reproved. Twenty-six lines become fifteen.",
        src:"theorem heapLocal_write_short (l : Loc) (e : Atom) : HeapLocal (.write l e) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  cases hex with\n  | write hl =>\n      have hl' : h l = _ := hl\n      have hdw : Heap.disjoint (Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)) hFrame := by\n        intro x\n        rcases hd x with hx | hx\n        · by_cases hxl : x = l\n          · subst hxl; rw [hl'] at hx; exact absurd hx (by simp)\n          · left; rw [write_other h l x (e.eval σ) hxl]; exact hx\n        · exact Or.inr hx\n      exact ⟨hdw, ⟨σ, Heap.write (Heap.union h hFrame) l (e.eval σ)⟩,\n             Exec.write (union_of_some hFrame hl'), rfl,\n             write_union_no_disjointness h hFrame l (e.eval σ)⟩"},
       {t:'p', h:"The <code>show</code> disappears with the <code>refine</code>, because the last slot of a tuple is checked up to reduction and the lemma&rsquo;s statement matches the projected goal without any help. <code>write_union_no_disjointness</code> is in scope in your editor, so this version compiles if you type it — and it puts the asymmetry into the shape of the proof: seven lines for the obligation that spends both hypotheses, one for the obligation that spends none."}
     ],
     pitfall:"Using <code>hl</code> where the proof uses <code>hl'</code>. It is the natural thing to do — <code>hl</code> is right there and says what you want — and it works everywhere except the one place it does not. <code>union_of_some hFrame hl</code> typechecks and builds the derivation; <code>rw [hl] at hx</code> fails with <code>Did not find an occurrence of the pattern</code>, quoting <code>{ store := σ, heap := h }.heap x</code> as the pattern and <code>h x = none</code> as the target. The rule is that a term-level application compares types up to reduction and a rewrite compares them syntactically, so the same hypothesis can be usable in one position and not in the other. The fix is the <code>have</code>; make the restatement once at the top rather than at the point of failure. &middot; The second one costs more time, because it does not fail where you make it. In the <code>x ≠ l</code> branch of the disjointness proof the goal is still the whole disjunction, and it is tempting to go straight at it with <code>rw [write_other h l x (e.eval σ) hxl]; exact hx</code>, leaving out the <code>left</code>. The <code>rw</code> succeeds — it happily rewrites inside the left disjunct — and the error lands on the <i>next</i> tactic: <code>Type mismatch</code>, <code>hx</code> has type <code>h x = none</code> but is expected to have type <code>h x = none ∨ hFrame x = none</code>. Read literally that message looks like a claim that <code>hx</code> is the wrong hypothesis; it is not, and the missing line is the one above.",
     variants:"Delete the disjointness hypothesis from <code>HeapLocal</code> altogether, and drop the conjunct it pays for, and <code>write</code> is still local. The property with no hypothesis and no first conjunct is provable for <code>write</code>, and its proof is the tuple above with the first slot removed — <code>write_union_no_disjointness</code> asks for nothing, and <code>union_of_some</code> asks only for the rule&rsquo;s own premise. Exactly one thing breaks, and it is the conjunct: <code>Heap.disjoint (Heap.write h l (e.eval σ)) hFrame</code> is unprovable without <code>hd</code>, since with <code>h</code> empty, <code>hFrame = Heap.singleton 0 9</code> and a write at 0 it is false. &middot; Put the write on the frame&rsquo;s side of the union instead — <code>Heap.write (Heap.union h hFrame) l v = Heap.union h (Heap.write hFrame l v)</code> — and it is false whenever <code>h</code> owns <code>l</code>: with <code>h = Heap.singleton 0 7</code>, <code>hFrame = Heap.empty</code>, <code>l = 0</code> and <code>v = 1</code>, the left side answers <code>some 1</code> at 0 and the right answers <code>some 7</code>, because the left-biased union reads from <code>h</code> and never reaches the write. Adding disjointness does not save it, and the reflex is to assume it does: <code>Heap.empty</code> is disjoint from everything, so the witness just given is already a disjoint pair. What the mirrored equation actually needs is <code>h l = none</code> — the frame&rsquo;s write is reachable exactly when <code>h</code> is silent at the address — and under that hypothesis it is provable, by the same <code>funext</code> and split, with the branches taken on <code>h x</code> as before. That hypothesis is the negation of the one the write rule gives you, which is why no rearrangement of <code>heapLocal_write</code> can use the mirrored form: the asymmetry is a property of <code>Heap.union</code> and of who owns <code>l</code>, not of writing. &middot; Give <code>write_other</code> the disequality the other way round — <code>l ≠ x</code> where it wants <code>x ≠ l</code> — and Lean reports <code>Application type mismatch</code>, saying that the argument <code>hlx</code> has type <code>l ≠ x</code> but is expected to have type <code>x ≠ l</code>, in the application <code>write_other h l x v hlx</code>. <code>Ne</code> records which side is which; <code>hxl.symm</code> is the repair, and here <code>by_cases</code> already produced the orientation the lemma wants."
    },

    /* ================================================ free === */

    {t:'sec', s:'free, where the two heaps meet'},

    {t:'p', h:"The <code>free</code> proof is the write proof with <code>Heap.erase</code> for <code>Heap.write</code> — twenty-nine lines against twenty-six — and for most of its length that substitution is all there is to it. The disjointness obligation gets easier: erasing removes a cell and never adds one, so if <code>h</code> was silent at an address then <code>Heap.erase h l</code> is silent there too, and the branch where the write proof had to derive a contradiction is here one call to <code>erase_same</code>. That half never consults the rule&rsquo;s premise at all."},

    {t:'p', h:"The equation is <code>Heap.erase (Heap.union h hFrame) l = Heap.union (Heap.erase h l) hFrame</code>, and away from <code>l</code> it goes exactly as the write equation did: the erase is invisible at every other address, both sides reduce to lookups in the same two heaps, and the split on <code>h x</code> with a <code>.trans</code> in each branch finishes it. At <code>l</code> it does not go as the write equation did, and the difference is not a matter of degree. Pick the proof up there, after <code>funext x</code>, the split on the address, and the <code>subst</code> that puts <code>x</code> where <code>l</code> was."},

    {t:'trace', title:'The erased address',
     start:'⊢ (h.union hFrame).erase x x = (h.erase x).union hFrame x',
     steps:[
       {tac:'rw [erase_same (Heap.union h hFrame) x]',
        state:"case pos\nx : Loc\nhl' : h x = some v✝\n⊢ none = (h.erase x).union hFrame x",
        h:"Context omitted and unchanged, as above. The left-hand side is gone in one step: erasing the combined heap at <code>x</code> and reading <code>x</code> back gives <code>none</code>, whatever was underneath."},
       {tac:'rw [union_of_none hFrame (erase_same h x)]',
        state:"case pos\nx : Loc\nhl' : h x = some v✝\n⊢ none = hFrame x",
        h:"The right-hand union&rsquo;s left argument holds nothing at this address — <code>erase_same h x</code> is the proof — so the union answers from the frame. And there the proof stops being about erasure."},
       {tac:'rcases hd x with hx | hx',
        state:"case pos.inl\nx : Loc\nhl' : h x = some v✝\nhx : h x = none\n⊢ none = hFrame x",
        h:"Nothing about heaps or commands can close <code>none = hFrame x</code>. It is a claim about the frame, and the only thing in the context that says anything about the frame is <code>hd</code>. Its first branch contradicts <code>hl'</code>, exactly as in the write proof."},
       {tac:'· rw [hx] at hl\'; exact absurd hl\' (by simp)  ·  exact hx.symm',
        state:'No goals.',
        h:"The two branches. In the first, <code>h x = none</code> against <code>hl' : h x = some v✝</code>; in the second, <code>hx : hFrame x = none</code> is the goal with its sides the wrong way round, and <code>.symm</code> turns it over."}
     ],
     done:'No goals.'},

    {t:'p', h:"<code>⊢ none = hFrame x</code> is the theorem. Everything on either side of it is bookkeeping about erasure; that one line is the claim that the frame owns nothing at the address being freed, and it is the reason the disjointness hypothesis is in the definition of <code>HeapLocal</code> rather than left to the caller as an afterthought. The table at the top of this page predicted this cell, and here it is: an equation that is <i>false</i> without <code>hd</code>, not merely unprovable."},

    {t:'p', h:"The difference between the two commands is testable. Take the hypothesis away — quantify over every frame, disjoint or not, and ask only for the run and the two equations — and one of the two commands still satisfies the property."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:"Locality with the disjointness hypothesis deleted and the disjointness conclusion deleted with it. <code>write</code> has it. <code>free</code> does not, and the counterexample is two one-cell heaps at the same address.",
     src:"def HeapLocalAnyFrame (c : Cmd) : Prop :=\n  ∀ σ h hFrame s',\n    Exec c ⟨σ, h⟩ s' →\n    ∃ r : State,\n      Exec c ⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩ r ∧\n      r.store = s'.store ∧\n      r.heap = Heap.union s'.heap hFrame\n\ntheorem heapLocalAnyFrame_write (l : Loc) (e : Atom) : HeapLocalAnyFrame (.write l e) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hex\n  cases hex with\n  | write hl =>\n      have hl' : h l = _ := hl\n      exact ⟨⟨σ, Heap.write (Heap.union h hFrame) l (e.eval σ)⟩,\n             Exec.write (union_of_some hFrame hl'), rfl,\n             write_union_no_disjointness h hFrame l (e.eval σ)⟩\n\ntheorem not_heapLocalAnyFrame_free : ¬ HeapLocalAnyFrame (.free 0) := by\n  intro hall\n  obtain ⟨r, hr, _, heq⟩ :=\n    hall (fun _ => 0) (Heap.singleton 0 7) (Heap.singleton 0 99)\n      ⟨fun _ => 0, Heap.erase (Heap.singleton 0 7) 0⟩\n      (Exec.free (singleton_same 0 7))\n  cases hr\n  have heq' : Heap.erase (Heap.union (Heap.singleton 0 7) (Heap.singleton 0 99)) 0\n            = Heap.union (Heap.erase (Heap.singleton 0 7) 0) (Heap.singleton 0 99) := heq\n  have h0 := congrFun heq' 0\n  rw [erase_same,\n      union_of_none (Heap.singleton 0 99) (erase_same (Heap.singleton 0 7) 0),\n      singleton_same] at h0\n  exact absurd h0 (by simp)"},

    {t:'p', h:"Follow the counterexample at address 0. <code>h</code> holds 7 there and the frame holds 99. The combined heap answers 7, because the union is left-biased and the frame&rsquo;s cell is hidden behind <code>h</code>&rsquo;s. The big run frees address 0 of the combined heap and leaves nothing there. The small run frees address 0 of <code>h</code>, and then <i>putting the frame beside the result uncovers the 99</i> — the cell that was hidden is now the only one, so <code>Heap.union s'.heap hFrame</code> answers <code>some 99</code>. One route ends with the address empty and the other with 99 in it, so the last equation of <code>HeapLocalAnyFrame</code> is false, and no choice of <code>r</code> repairs it, since <code>Exec.free</code> determines the final heap."},

    {t:'p', h:"Read that as a sentence about programs and it says something the definition alone does not: a command may free memory it owns, and the frame rule may carry any assertion past it, precisely because ownership was exclusive. Sharing one cell between the command and the frame would let <code>free</code> destroy an assertion it never mentions. The disjointness hypothesis is not a technicality of the model — it is the ownership discipline, appearing as the one goal that would otherwise not close."},

    /* ================================================= exercise m8-3 === */

    {t:'ex',
     id:'m8-3',
     name:'heapLocal_free',
     hard:true,
     why:"The case where the two heaps genuinely meet, and the last pointwise heap argument you will write. After this, locality is closed under sequencing, conditionals and loops by three proofs that never open a heap at an address, and the frame rule follows from the collection. This theorem is then cited by name three times: once in Unit 27 and twice in Unit 29, each time to frame a deallocation. It is also the only one of the five command-locality proofs whose disjointness hypothesis is spent inside an equation rather than handed back as one, which is what the counterexample above measures.",
     setup:"The same shape as <code>heapLocal_write</code> and the same decomposition: a <code>have</code> for the disjointness, then the <code>refine</code>, then a <code>show</code>, then <code>funext</code> and a split. Two things move. The disjointness half gets shorter — <code>left</code> can be committed to before the split on <code>x = l</code>, because erasing never makes the left disjunct false — and the equation half gets longer at exactly one address, where you will meet <code>⊢ none = hFrame x</code> and have to reach for <code>hd</code> a second time.",
     goal:"theorem heapLocal_free (l : Loc) : HeapLocal (.free l) := by",
     hints:[
       "Unfolded, with nothing yet done to it: for every <code>σ</code>, <code>h</code>, <code>hFrame</code> and <code>s'</code>, if <code>hFrame</code> is disjoint from <code>h</code> and <code>free l</code> takes <code>⟨σ, h⟩</code> to <code>s'</code>, then <code>s'.heap</code> is still disjoint from <code>hFrame</code>, and some state <code>r</code> is reached by the same command from <code>⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩</code> with <code>r.store = s'.store</code> and <code>r.heap = Heap.union s'.heap hFrame</code>. As in <code>m8-2</code>, nothing in the statement says what <code>s'</code> is.",
       "<code>Exec</code> has one rule for <code>free</code>, so <code>s'</code> has store <code>σ</code> and heap <code>Heap.erase h l</code>, and the rule hands you its premise: <code>h</code> holds a value at <code>l</code>. Disjointness: erasing can only remove cells, so wherever <code>h</code> was silent the erased heap is silent, and at the erased address it is silent for free. The left disjunct always holds, whichever way the split on the address goes — so unlike the write proof, there is no branch to refute and the premise is never touched. Equation: away from <code>l</code> it is the write argument with <code>erase</code> for <code>write</code>. At <code>l</code> the left side collapses to <code>none</code> and the right side collapses to whatever the frame holds, and closing that gap is where the disjointness hypothesis is spent a second time, against the free rule&rsquo;s premise.",
       "<code>cases hex with | free hl =&gt;</code>, then the same <code>have hl' : h l = _ := hl</code>. The disjointness sub-proof is <code>intro x</code>, <code>rcases hd x</code>, <code>left</code>, <code>by_cases</code>, then <code>erase_same</code> in one branch and <code>erase_other</code> in the other. The equation needs <code>erase_same</code>, <code>erase_other</code>, <code>union_of_none</code>, <code>union_of_some</code> and, at the erased address, <code>rcases hd x</code> again with <code>absurd</code> on one side and <code>.symm</code> on the other.",
       "<code>intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex</code> / <code>cases hex with | free hl =&gt;</code> / <code>have hl' : h l = _ := hl</code>, then <code>have hde : Heap.disjoint (Heap.erase h l) hFrame := by</code> with <code>intro x</code> and <code>rcases hd x with hx | hx</code> under it. Once <code>hde</code> exists, <code>refine ⟨hde, ⟨σ, Heap.erase (Heap.union h hFrame) l⟩, Exec.free (union_of_some hFrame hl'), rfl, ?_⟩</code>, then <code>show Heap.erase (Heap.union h hFrame) l = Heap.union (Heap.erase h l) hFrame</code>, and the goal is a heap equation like any other."
     ],
     sol:"theorem heapLocal_free (l : Loc) : HeapLocal (.free l) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  cases hex with\n  | free hl =>\n      have hl' : h l = _ := hl\n      have hde : Heap.disjoint (Heap.erase h l) hFrame := by\n        intro x\n        rcases hd x with hx | hx\n        · left\n          by_cases hxl : x = l\n          · subst hxl; exact erase_same h x\n          · rw [erase_other h l x hxl]; exact hx\n        · exact Or.inr hx\n      refine ⟨hde, ⟨σ, Heap.erase (Heap.union h hFrame) l⟩,\n              Exec.free (union_of_some hFrame hl'), rfl, ?_⟩\n      show Heap.erase (Heap.union h hFrame) l = Heap.union (Heap.erase h l) hFrame\n      funext x\n      by_cases hxl : x = l\n      · subst hxl\n        rw [erase_same (Heap.union h hFrame) x,\n            union_of_none hFrame (erase_same h x)]\n        rcases hd x with hx | hx\n        · rw [hx] at hl'; exact absurd hl' (by simp)\n        · exact hx.symm\n      · rw [erase_other (Heap.union h hFrame) l x hxl]\n        have hex' : Heap.erase h l x = h x := erase_other h l x hxl\n        cases hx : h x with\n        | none => rw [union_of_none hFrame hx, union_of_none hFrame (hex'.trans hx)]\n        | some w => rw [union_of_some hFrame hx, union_of_some hFrame (hex'.trans hx)]",
     solNote:"Three lines longer than <code>heapLocal_write</code>, and the extra work sits in one place: the second <code>rcases hd x</code> and its two branches, inside the equation, at the erased address. Nothing else in the proof asks who owns what.",
     expl:"Everything except the erased address is the write proof with one operation exchanged for another. The disjointness half is shorter because <code>Heap.erase</code> only ever makes the left disjunct easier, so no branch is vacuous and <code>left</code> can be committed to before the split. The equation half is longer because at <code>l</code> the left side collapses to <code>none</code> while the right side collapses to a lookup in the frame, and closing that gap is not bookkeeping: it is the statement that the frame does not own the cell being freed.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'intro σ h hFrame s\' hd hex', h:"The same opening line as every locality proof in this module: four binders and two implications discharged, leaving the conjunction."},
       {tac:'cases hex with | free hl =>', h:"One rule for <code>Exec (.free l)</code>, so one branch. <code>s'</code> is replaced by the state with the store unchanged and the heap <code>Heap.erase h l</code>; <code>hl</code> is the premise, with the erased value inaccessible as <code>v✝</code>."},
       {tac:'have hl\' : h l = _ := hl', h:"Same restatement as in the write proof and for the same reason: <code>rw</code> will not see through <code>{ store := σ, heap := h }.heap</code>. Here it is used twice — once as the argument to <code>union_of_some</code>, once inside the equation, at the erased address."},
       {tac:'have hde : Heap.disjoint (Heap.erase h l) hFrame := by', h:"The first obligation, opened separately. This is the half that costs nothing: it needs <code>hd</code> and nothing else."},
       {tac:'intro x; rcases hd x with hx | hx', h:"Fixed an address and used the hypothesis there, exactly as in the write proof. The second branch, <code>hFrame x = none</code>, closes at once with <code>exact Or.inr hx</code>: erasing does not touch the frame."},
       {tac:'· left', h:"Committed to the left disjunct before knowing whether <code>x</code> is <code>l</code>. In the write proof that would have been premature — at the address written, the left disjunct is false. Erasing never defines an address, so here it is safe whichever way the split goes, and it saves the branch."},
       {tac:'by_cases hxl : x = l', h:"Split the address, under the <code>left</code> rather than above it. The goal is now the single equation <code>h.erase l x = none</code> in both sub-branches, and the split is made only because the two lookup laws for <code>Heap.erase</code> demand it — not because the two cases are answered by different kinds of argument, which is what forced the split in the write proof."},
       {tac:'· subst hxl; exact erase_same h x', h:"At the erased address, the erased heap holds nothing, by a law that needs no hypothesis at all. The context here is contradictory — <code>hx : h x = none</code> against <code>hl' : h x = some v✝</code> — and none of that is used, because the goal is true outright."},
       {tac:'· rw [erase_other h l x hxl]; exact hx', h:"Away from it, the erase is invisible and the goal becomes the hypothesis <code>hx : h x = none</code>."},
       {tac:'refine ⟨hde, ⟨σ, Heap.erase (Heap.union h hFrame) l⟩, Exec.free (union_of_some hFrame hl\'), rfl, ?_⟩', h:"The state claimed for the big run is the big heap with <code>l</code> erased. <code>union_of_some hFrame hl'</code> supplies <code>Exec.free</code>&rsquo;s premise about that heap: the union answers at <code>l</code> whatever <code>h</code> answers, and <code>h</code> answers <code>some v✝</code>."},
       {tac:'show Heap.erase (Heap.union h hFrame) l = Heap.union (Heap.erase h l) hFrame', h:"The remaining goal, restated without the projections. From here it is an equation between heaps and nothing about commands survives in it."},
       {tac:'funext x', h:"Turned the equation between two heaps into an equation between two lookups at an arbitrary address, and gave the proof an <code>x</code> to split on."},
       {tac:'by_cases hxl : x = l; subst hxl', h:"Split the address into the one being freed and all the others, and in the positive branch replaced <code>l</code> by <code>x</code> throughout — including inside <code>hl'</code>, which now reads <code>h x = some v✝</code>. That substitution is what makes the contradiction two lines below available; without it <code>hl'</code> would still be about <code>l</code> and the branch would have nothing to say about <code>x</code>."},
       {tac:'rw [erase_same (Heap.union h hFrame) x, union_of_none hFrame (erase_same h x)]', h:"The erased address, both sides at once. Left: <code>none</code>. Right: the union&rsquo;s left argument is undefined here, so it answers from the frame. What is left is <code>⊢ none = hFrame x</code>."},
       {tac:'rcases hd x with hx | hx', h:"The second and last consultation of the disjointness hypothesis, and the only one in the whole unit that is inside an equation rather than a disjointness claim."},
       {tac:'· rw [hx] at hl\'; exact absurd hl\' (by simp)', h:"The branch where <code>h</code> is silent at <code>x</code>. It contradicts the free rule&rsquo;s premise: a command cannot free what it does not hold. Rewriting turns <code>hl'</code> into <code>none = some v✝</code>."},
       {tac:'· exact hx.symm', h:"The branch where the frame is silent. That is the goal, with its sides swapped, so <code>Eq.symm</code> in dot notation turns it over. <code>exact hx</code> alone fails."},
       {tac:'rw [erase_other (Heap.union h hFrame) l x hxl]', h:"Every address other than <code>l</code>. Stripped the erase off the left-hand side: away from the address erased, an erase answers whatever was there before, so the goal&rsquo;s left side becomes <code>h.union hFrame x</code>. The right-hand side keeps its erase, buried inside a union where no single lookup law reaches it."},
       {tac:'have hex\' : Heap.erase h l x = h x := erase_other h l x hxl', h:"The same law about the small heap, saved as a term for the same reason it was in <code>m8-2</code>: the erase on the right sits under the union as an argument and is applied to nothing, so <code>rw</code> cannot find it, while <code>.trans</code> takes it happily."},
       {tac:'cases hx : h x with | none => … | some w => …', h:"Both sides are unions again, so split on whether <code>h</code> is defined at <code>x</code>, keeping the answer as <code>hx</code>, and resolve each union by whichever lookup law applies. <code>hex'.trans hx</code> transports that answer from <code>h</code> to <code>Heap.erase h l</code>, which is what the right-hand union needs. Line for line this is the write proof with one operation exchanged for another, and no hypothesis is consulted in any of it."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'trace', title:'The disjointness obligation, which costs nothing',
        start:"⊢ (h.erase l).disjoint hFrame",
        steps:[
          {tac:'intro x  ·  rcases hd x with hx | hx  ·  left',
           state:"case inl\nx : Loc\nhx : h x = none\n⊢ h.erase l x = none",
           h:"Every context line except the ones shown is omitted throughout — including the unrestated <code>hl</code>, which travels through the <code>subst</code> below exactly as <code>hl'</code> does. Three tactics, and the goal is already down to one equation: fix an address, take the branch in which <code>h</code> is silent, and claim the erased heap is silent too."},
          {tac:'by_cases hxl : x = l  ·  subst hxl',
           state:"case pos\nx : Loc\nhx : h x = none\nhl' : h x = some v✝\n⊢ h.erase x x = none",
           h:"At the erased address. The context is contradictory here — <code>hx</code> and <code>hl'</code> disagree — and it does not matter, because the goal is true anyway. That is the difference from the write proof, where the corresponding branch had to be shown impossible before anything could be said."},
          {tac:'exact erase_same h x',
           state:'No goals.',
           h:"One law from Unit 05, with no hypothesis and no case analysis under it."}
        ],
        done:'No goals.'},
       {t:'p', h:"Set the two disjointness proofs side by side and the difference is where <code>left</code> sits. Both open <code>intro x</code> and <code>rcases hd x</code>. The <code>free</code> version then says <code>left</code> and proves one equation twice over; the <code>write</code> version cannot say <code>left</code> yet, because at the address written the left disjunct is false, and it has to spend the rule&rsquo;s premise on showing that the branch cannot arise. Committing to <code>left</code> first in the write proof does in fact compile — <code>absurd</code> closes a goal of any shape, including one that is false — but the branch is then being closed by contradiction while pretending to prove the disjunct, which is a worse description of what is happening."},
       {t:'state', cap:"The goal after the two rewrites at the erased address, quoted with the six other context lines omitted and unchanged. Nothing about <code>free</code>, nothing about erasure, nothing about the command at all: the whole locality of deallocation comes down to this.",
        src:"case pos\nx : Loc\nhl' : h x = some v✝\n⊢ none = hFrame x"}
     ],
     pitfall:"Closing <code>⊢ none = hFrame x</code> with <code>exact hx</code>. The hypothesis is <code>hx : hFrame x = none</code> and the goal is the same equation written the other way round, so Lean reports <code>Type mismatch</code>, saying that <code>hx</code> has type <code>hFrame x = none</code> but is expected to have type <code>none = hFrame x</code>. Propositional equality is symmetric and Lean still distinguishes the two statements, because they are different types with different proofs; <code>hx.symm</code> is the one asked for. The orientation is not the author&rsquo;s choice either — it comes out of the rewrite two lines earlier, which replaced the left-hand side by <code>none</code> and left the frame lookup where it stood.",
     variants:"Let the frame own the freed cell — drop <code>hd</code> from the definition, and the conclusion about disjointness with it — and locality fails. With <code>h = Heap.singleton 0 7</code> and <code>hFrame = Heap.singleton 0 99</code>, the combined heap answers <code>some 7</code> at 0 because the union is left-biased, so the big run erases the one visible cell and leaves the address empty; the small run erases <code>h</code>&rsquo;s cell, and putting the frame beside the result uncovers the 99. Final heaps <code>none</code> and <code>some 99</code> at the same address; the theorem <code>not_heapLocalAnyFrame_free</code> above is that argument, compiled. The same deletion leaves <code>write</code> local, which is what makes this a fact about <code>free</code> rather than about the definition. &middot; Weaken the free rule so that it does not require <code>h l = some v</code> — deallocate anything, allocated or not — and the equation is false <i>even for disjoint heaps</i>. Take <code>h = Heap.empty</code> and <code>hFrame = Heap.singleton 0 5</code>, which are disjoint, and free 0: the big run erases the frame&rsquo;s cell out of the combined heap and the small run leaves it standing, so the two sides answer <code>none</code> and <code>some 5</code>. Disjointness alone does not save the equation; it saves it only in company with the premise, which is why the goal <code>⊢ none = hFrame x</code> needs both. &middot; Swap the two heaps in the union — claim <code>Heap.erase (Heap.union hFrame h) l = Heap.union hFrame (Heap.erase h l)</code> — and it is again true, and again only with both <code>hd</code> and the rule&rsquo;s premise: drop the premise and <code>h = Heap.empty</code>, <code>hFrame = Heap.singleton 0 5</code> refutes it just as it refutes the original. What moves is where disjointness is spent and what it buys. Here the erased address is the one where the <i>right-hand</i> union has <code>hFrame</code> as its left argument, so what you need from <code>hd</code> is that <code>hFrame</code> is silent at <code>l</code> — and then the union falls through to <code>Heap.erase h l</code>, which is silent as well, so both sides read <code>none</code> and the goal never becomes <code>none = hFrame x</code> at all. Away from <code>l</code> the proof does change: the split is on <code>hFrame x</code> rather than <code>h x</code>, because that is now the union&rsquo;s left argument, and <code>.trans</code> disappears — the two unions there share their left argument outright, so there is nothing to transport."
    },

    /* ============================================================ close === */

    {t:'note', kind:'key', title:'What the two proofs say that the definition does not',
     h:"A hypothesis can be in a theorem for two different reasons. <code>write</code> needs disjointness to <i>return</i> disjointness: nothing in its equation consults the hypothesis, and the equation is true of overlapping heaps. <code>free</code> needs disjointness to make its equation true, and without it the theorem is false with a two-cell counterexample. When you meet a proof obligation you cannot close, the question worth asking is which of the two kinds it is — a fact you must hand back, or a fact the mathematics depends on. Unit 24 read the extra conjunct off a goal of the first kind — the frame proof needed disjointness handed back so that it could rebuild the star. This unit is where that conjunct is paid, and exactly one of the two payments costs anything."},

    {t:'dod', h:"You can decompose a locality goal into the disjointness half and the equation half before writing Lean, and say for each command which hypotheses each half spends. You can prove a heap equation across a union by <code>funext</code>, a split on the address, and the four lookup laws, resolving each union with <code>union_of_none</code> or <code>union_of_some</code> and composing two equations with <code>.trans</code> where one heap&rsquo;s answer has to be transported to another. You can restate a hypothesis that <code>rw</code> refuses, with <code>have h' : T := e</code> and an underscore where a name is inaccessible, and say why <code>exact</code> would not have needed it. You can use <code>show</code> to replace a goal written through structure projections by the equation it is. And you can say, with a counterexample, what goes wrong if a command is allowed to free memory the frame also holds."},

    {t:'p', h:"Five commands are local. A program is not five commands — it is commands glued together with <code>;;</code>, and three of <code>Cmd</code>&rsquo;s eight constructors are control flow whose locality nothing has yet claimed. Sequencing is where the extra conjunct is finally spent rather than only established: the first command hands its disjointness to the second, and without it the second could not be run on the big heap at all."}

  ]
});
